1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,837
A bright red heater warms you for
hours, and never gives you a sunburn.

2
00:00:04,837 --> 00:00:07,169
A few minutes of strong sunlight can.

3
00:00:07,569 --> 00:00:12,114
A photo darkroom glows red, because
red light does not spoil the photo

4
00:00:12,114 --> 00:00:13,014
paper.

5
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:15,982
Aarav noticed it after
a long ride at noon.

6
00:00:15,982 --> 00:00:19,568
Burnt arms, yet the red
heater at home never did that.

7
00:00:19,918 --> 00:00:22,744
The secret is not how bright the light is.

8
00:00:22,744 --> 00:00:26,373
It is how much energy each
tiny packet of light carries.

9
00:00:26,873 --> 00:00:31,548
By the end, you will see why light acts
like a wave, and also like a stream

10
00:00:31,548 --> 00:00:32,498
of particles.

11
00:00:33,148 --> 00:00:35,380
So what happens when light hits a metal?

12
00:00:35,730 --> 00:00:38,634
In eighteen eighty seven,
Heinrich Hertz saw it.

13
00:00:38,634 --> 00:00:41,908
When light falls on certain
metals, electrons jump out.

14
00:00:42,308 --> 00:00:45,904
Metals like potassium,
rubidium and caesium do this.

15
00:00:45,904 --> 00:00:48,211
This is the photoelectric effect.

16
00:00:48,211 --> 00:00:49,606
Photo means light.

17
00:00:50,006 --> 00:00:53,378
The electrons that come out
are called photoelectrons.

18
00:00:53,978 --> 00:00:56,477
Let's test it on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:56,477 --> 00:00:59,120
A potassium plate, full of electrons.

20
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:01,505
First, a very bright red lamp.

21
00:01:01,505 --> 00:01:04,498
A flood of tiny packets
of light hits the plate.

22
00:01:04,898 --> 00:01:06,269
Nothing comes out.

23
00:01:06,269 --> 00:01:07,908
Make it even brighter.

24
00:01:07,908 --> 00:01:09,011
Still nothing.

25
00:01:09,411 --> 00:01:11,834
Now switch to a dim violet lamp.

26
00:01:11,834 --> 00:01:13,809
Electrons fly out at once.

27
00:01:13,809 --> 00:01:15,784
No waiting, no time lag.

28
00:01:16,184 --> 00:01:18,074
Make the violet light brighter.

29
00:01:18,074 --> 00:01:21,224
More electrons come out,
but each one is no faster.

30
00:01:21,624 --> 00:01:24,820
Now raise the frequency,
towards ultraviolet.

31
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:26,861
Each electron leaves faster.

32
00:01:27,511 --> 00:01:28,798
So, three rules.

33
00:01:28,798 --> 00:01:31,769
One, electrons leave the
moment light strikes.

34
00:01:31,769 --> 00:01:34,441
Two, brighter light gives more electrons.

35
00:01:34,441 --> 00:01:38,312
Three, below a certain frequency,
nothing comes out at all.

36
00:01:38,712 --> 00:01:42,357
That cut off is called the
threshold frequency, nu zero.

37
00:01:42,357 --> 00:01:44,057
Every metal has its own.

38
00:01:44,457 --> 00:01:46,316
Two lamps, side by side.

39
00:01:46,316 --> 00:01:48,073
One very bright and red.

40
00:01:48,073 --> 00:01:49,881
The other dim and violet.

41
00:01:50,281 --> 00:01:51,335
Pause and think.

42
00:01:51,335 --> 00:01:54,026
Which lamp can free an
electron from the plate?

43
00:01:57,226 --> 00:01:58,730
The dim violet lamp!

44
00:01:58,730 --> 00:02:01,000
Brightness only adds more packets.

45
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,845
Each red packet is too weak,
and packets cannot team up.

46
00:02:05,495 --> 00:02:08,919
Albert Einstein explained
this in nineteen oh five.

47
00:02:08,919 --> 00:02:10,046
Step by step.

48
00:02:10,396 --> 00:02:11,296
Step one.

49
00:02:11,249 --> 00:02:13,859
Light comes in packets called photons.

50
00:02:13,859 --> 00:02:15,732
Each carries energy h nu.

51
00:02:15,732 --> 00:02:18,831
h is Planck's constant,
nu is the frequency.

52
00:02:19,581 --> 00:02:20,481
Step two.

53
00:02:20,434 --> 00:02:24,723
One photon hits one electron,
and gives it all of its energy.

54
00:02:25,473 --> 00:02:26,373
Step three.

55
00:02:26,345 --> 00:02:30,004
Part of that energy pulls the
electron free from the metal.

56
00:02:30,004 --> 00:02:32,838
This part is the work function, h nu zero.

57
00:02:33,588 --> 00:02:34,500
Step four.

58
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:38,538
Whatever is left becomes kinetic
energy, half m v squared.

59
00:02:39,288 --> 00:02:43,198
So, h nu equals h nu zero,
plus half m v squared.

60
00:02:43,198 --> 00:02:45,088
Every term is in joules.

61
00:02:45,838 --> 00:02:47,045
Remember this.

62
00:02:47,045 --> 00:02:49,809
Brightness decides how many electrons.

63
00:02:49,809 --> 00:02:51,885
Frequency decides how fast.

64
00:02:52,585 --> 00:02:54,321
Now, one worked example.

65
00:02:54,621 --> 00:02:59,613
A metal has a threshold frequency of
five times ten to the fourteen hertz.

66
00:02:59,613 --> 00:03:03,781
Violet light of wavelength four
hundred nanometres falls on it.

67
00:03:04,281 --> 00:03:07,366
Find the kinetic energy
of the photoelectrons.

68
00:03:07,866 --> 00:03:10,113
First, nu equals c over lambda.

69
00:03:10,113 --> 00:03:14,606
Three times ten to the eight, over
four hundred nanometres, gives seven

70
00:03:14,606 --> 00:03:17,469
point five times ten
to the fourteen hertz.

71
00:03:18,219 --> 00:03:22,193
Next, kinetic energy is h
times, nu minus nu zero.

72
00:03:22,193 --> 00:03:27,164
Seven point five minus five is two
point five times ten to the fourteen

73
00:03:27,164 --> 00:03:28,064
hertz.

74
00:03:28,597 --> 00:03:33,728
Multiply by six point six two six
times ten to the minus thirty four.

75
00:03:33,728 --> 00:03:38,746
The kinetic energy is one point six
six times ten to the minus nineteen

76
00:03:38,746 --> 00:03:39,646
joules.

77
00:03:40,242 --> 00:03:44,895
Above zero, so electrons do come out,
at about six times ten to the five

78
00:03:44,895 --> 00:03:46,197
metres per second.

79
00:03:46,847 --> 00:03:49,810
So is light a wave, or
a stream of particles?

80
00:03:50,210 --> 00:03:51,998
Some effects need a wave.

81
00:03:51,998 --> 00:03:56,638
Light spreads around tiny edges and
makes bright and dark bands: diffraction

82
00:03:56,638 --> 00:03:57,850
and interference.

83
00:03:58,250 --> 00:04:00,392
Other effects need particles.

84
00:04:00,392 --> 00:04:05,041
The photoelectric effect and black
body radiation only make sense with

85
00:04:05,041 --> 00:04:05,941
photons.

86
00:04:06,225 --> 00:04:08,272
So light has a dual behaviour.

87
00:04:08,272 --> 00:04:12,675
It travels like a wave, and it
gives up its energy like a particle.

88
00:04:13,075 --> 00:04:17,654
In nineteen twenty four, Louis de
Broglie asked: if light can be both,

89
00:04:17,654 --> 00:04:19,335
can matter be both too?

90
00:04:19,335 --> 00:04:20,353
He said yes.

91
00:04:20,353 --> 00:04:22,697
Even a moving electron has a wave.

92
00:04:23,347 --> 00:04:24,813
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:25,163 --> 00:04:28,916
Many students think that brighter
light gives faster electrons.

94
00:04:29,316 --> 00:04:30,477
It does not.

95
00:04:30,477 --> 00:04:33,015
Intensity changes only the number.

96
00:04:33,015 --> 00:04:36,014
Kinetic energy depends only on frequency.

97
00:04:36,664 --> 00:04:38,222
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:38,222 --> 00:04:42,820
As numericals on Einstein's equation,
and statement questions on intensity

99
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:43,938
and frequency.

100
00:04:44,538 --> 00:04:45,857
Exam level now.

101
00:04:45,857 --> 00:04:50,563
Light of frequency one point five
times the threshold ejects electrons

102
00:04:50,563 --> 00:04:52,340
with kinetic energy K.

103
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:53,995
Double the frequency.

104
00:04:53,995 --> 00:04:56,409
What is the new kinetic energy?

105
00:04:56,409 --> 00:04:57,784
Pause and solve.

106
00:05:03,184 --> 00:05:04,668
The answer is C.

107
00:05:04,668 --> 00:05:05,568
Four K.

108
00:05:05,996 --> 00:05:09,585
Kinetic energy is h
times, nu minus nu zero.

109
00:05:09,585 --> 00:05:11,943
So K is half of h nu zero.

110
00:05:12,443 --> 00:05:17,353
Doubled, nu is three nu zero, so
the kinetic energy is two h nu zero.

111
00:05:17,853 --> 00:05:19,734
Two over one half is four.

112
00:05:19,734 --> 00:05:20,798
So, four K.

113
00:05:20,798 --> 00:05:23,832
Option A, two K, forgets
the work function.

114
00:05:24,432 --> 00:05:25,893
Here is your recap.

115
00:05:25,893 --> 00:05:27,394
Screenshot this card.

116
00:05:30,594 --> 00:05:33,430
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

117
00:05:33,830 --> 00:05:34,730
One.

118
00:05:34,356 --> 00:05:39,061
Why can dim violet light free
electrons, when bright red light cannot?

119
00:05:39,861 --> 00:05:40,761
Two.

120
00:05:40,403 --> 00:05:43,463
What is the threshold
frequency of a metal?

121
00:05:44,263 --> 00:05:45,163
Three.

122
00:05:44,863 --> 00:05:48,501
Name one wave effect of light,
and one particle effect.

123
00:05:49,401 --> 00:05:53,548
Next time: if an electron is
a wave, how long is that wave?

124
00:05:53,548 --> 00:05:56,207
We find out in the de Broglie relation.
